column10:
What is that intended to mean?<p>Do you mean:<p><pre><code>  $var = $tom ? $tom : 'ed';
</code></pre>
If so, you can also do:<p><pre><code>  $var = $tom or $var = 'ed';
</code></pre>
If you don't want to calculate $tom twice.

column102:
I personally use a wraper around php's create_function to predefine upto 6 parameters and also return the last statement.<p><pre><code>  function lambda( $code )
  {
    if( !preg_match( '/return/', $code ) )
    {
      $code = preg_replace('/^(.*;(?!\s*$))?/s', "$1 return ", $code);
    }

    $code .= ';';
    $response = create_function('$_=null,$_1=null,$_2=null,$_3=null,$_4=null,$_5=null', $code);

    if( !$response )
    {
        error_log("lambda($code) failed");
    }
    return $response;
  }

</code></pre>
This converts:<p><pre><code>  $func = create_function('$v','return htmlenties($v,ENT_QUOTES);');
  array_map($func, $data);
</code></pre>
Into:<p><pre><code>  array_map(lambda('htmlenties($_, ENT_QUOTES)'), $data);</code></pre>
